--TEST--
`Dn::parse` reads text as a DN's structure and an attribute name selects what the server compares, so neither compiles with a `tainted` string, while a `tainted` value does — ADR 0278 § 7, `rule:core-classes/ldap-dn-is-the-launderer` and `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`
--FILE--
<?nvs
tainted string $text = "CN=Ann,DC=test";
var $fromInput = Core\Ldap\Dn::parse($text);
var $fromLog = Core\Ldap\Dn::parse(Core\Ldap\Dn::of("CN", "Ann")->toString());
var $built = Core\Ldap\Dn::of("CN", $text);
var $badName = Core\Ldap\Dn::of($text, "Ann");
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
  --> case.nvs:3:38
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
  --> case.nvs:4:36
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
  --> case.nvs:6:33
%A
